## Radical innovation — advisory, not applied

Delimit the write-rule section inside `prompt_text()` with markdown-invisible markers, e.g. `<!-- summem:write-rule v1 -->` … `<!-- /summem:write-rule -->` around the Register Memories body, and nothing else.

This costs two comment lines in the emitted prefix and changes no agent-facing prose (HTML comments do not render and read as noise to a model far less than a sentence does). What it buys is the one acknowledged casualty of the chosen design: the creative's tradeoff paragraph concedes that "the shipped default write rule no longer upgrades when someone copies a new script" and that operators "re-run `init` and merge by hand" with no way to see what changed. Delimiters give a later `summem init --diff` (or a plain `sed` recipe in the README) an exact, machine-findable span in a consumer's `AGENTS.md`: show the operator the shipped default's current text against the span they committed, and let them decide. It is a seam, not a mechanism — no store file, no overlay, no `summem upgrade`, none of what the overlay option was rejected for — and it is additive to the disjointness fence rather than in tension with it, because the markers bracket exactly the policy region the split just created.

The risk to weigh is that a marker in the prefix is a second mechanical artifact beyond the wake handoff, and the pre-mortem is explicit that mechanical creep into `AGENTS.md` is the way this design rots. A comment that carries no instruction is a weaker precedent than argv, but it is a precedent. Do not adopt it inside this task; it is a separate decision about the upgrade path.

